# Feasibility scoping (pre-plan), idea-review-repo

Basis: the objective, and the resources listed for this world. No plan or context brief existed when this was written; revise once `assignment.json` and `idea-repo` material exist.

## What must be true for the objective to be met
A NeurIPS-2026-style paper: (1) a theory of early-training JEPA collapse (per-direction growth-vs-decay near the collapsed solution, giving a survival condition and a rank prediction), (2) an account of what predictor design/scale, masking ratio and target-encoder update change in that condition, (3) empirical tests, (4) LaTeX source in NeurIPS template, compiled PDF, code and results.

## Resources and limits
- Sandbox: 8 CPU, 32 GB, no GPU. `local-compute`: same limits, no GPU, no network (fetch data/packages first).
- `ibex`: A100/V100 GPUs, shared, queue unpredictable; use only for runs that need it and track jobs.
- Model-time budget is shared by 8 positions (1e6 s); no time limit per turn but idle turns are cut.
- Anything first published after 2026-09-01 is off-limits.

## Main risks
1. Theory scope creep. A fully general theorem for deep nonlinear encoders is not achievable. Bound the claim: analysis of the dynamics linearised around the collapsed point (deep-linear / linear-encoder JEPA with predictor and EMA target), with the survival criterion stated as a condition on the per-direction growth rate vs decay rate. Say plainly which results are exact for the linear model and which are only empirical for nonlinear networks.
2. Theory-vs-experiment mismatch. Real ViT/ResNet I-JEPA at ImageNet scale is infeasible here. Test in tiers: (a) synthetic/linear model where the prediction is quantitative (predicted vs measured threshold, rank, per-direction trajectories); (b) small nonlinear encoders (MLP / small CNN) on a small dataset (e.g. CIFAR-10 / MNIST-scale or synthetic), CPU-feasible; (c) optionally a small ViT-style JEPA on a GPU only if tier (a)-(b) are finished. Do not claim ImageNet-scale validation.
3. Heuristic coverage. Each heuristic (predictor depth/width/scale, mask ratio, EMA rate / stop-gradient) must map to a term in the theory before it is swept; otherwise the "what the heuristics change" claim is ungrounded. Limit to those three families.
4. Collapse measurement. Define rank/collapse metric up front (effective rank of embedding covariance, singular-value spectrum, per-direction growth), with several seeds. Collapse is decided early, so short runs (few epochs, many seeds) are enough and cheaper than long ones; budget seeds over model size.
5. Cost of sweeps. Grid size = heuristics x values x seeds. Keep each grid cell cheap (seconds-minutes on CPU for tier a-b); decide the sweep sizes against a measured per-run time from a pilot before launching.
6. Prior-work baselines: cite related theory (e.g. linear BYOL/SimSiam/DirectPred dynamics, JEPA/I-JEPA/V-JEPA, VICReg) only from work published before 2026-09-01; do not claim novelty without idea-repo's check of what is already established. Do not reproduce baseline numbers that are not re-run.
7. Paper deliverable: compile with TeX Live 2026 and the NeurIPS template (neurips.cc reachable); 9-page main-text limit means a bounded set of figures/tables (suggest <= 4 figures, <= 2 tables).

## Recommended bounds for the plan
- One core theorem/proposition set on the linearised model + one numerical verification of it.
- Tier (b) experiments as the nonlinear check; state negative or partial agreement honestly.
- Pilot first (single run timings, whether collapse vs non-collapse actually occurs in the chosen setup), then fix sweep sizes.
- Any run > a few minutes goes through `local-compute`/`ibex`, not the turn shell.

## Judgement
Feasible at the bounded scope above; not feasible at the scope "validated on large-scale JEPA pretraining". I will judge the actual plan against this when `story-repo` posts it.

