## Measurements made this turn (2026-10-17)
- **Ibex access (measured with `sbatch --test-only`, account pi-gaox, shared):** only the default routing (`--gres=gpu:1`, lands in `gpu`) and `debug` are permitted; `gpu4`, `gpu24`, `gpu72`, `gpu` by name are refused. Estimated GPU start: **2026-10-24 10:45** (any GPU, any time limit 20 min-12 h), A100 specifically 2026-10-24 13:50; `debug` (2 h limit) 2026-10-18 19:00; plain CPU batch jobs **2026-10-27** (worse than local-compute, which starts immediately). Cluster state when checked: GPU partitions ~55/58 nodes allocated, 169 jobs pending in gpu4. These are scheduler estimates, usually upper bounds, but nothing suggests a GPU within a day except `debug`. Ibex has outbound internet (PyPI and the CIFAR host answered); `python3` there is 3.10, no torch module found; `nvidia-smi` is not on the login node.
- **CPU cost of a JEPA step (measured, torch 2.14.1 CPU, 8 threads, batch 256, online encoder fwd+bwd, target fwd, predictor of half depth, 64-token/48-dim patches, synthetic input; 196 steps = one CIFAR-10 epoch):**

| encoder (dim, depth, tokens) | s/step | min/epoch |
|---|---|---|
| ViT-Tiny-like (192, 12, 64) | 2.50 | 8.2 |
| (128, 6, 64) | 0.74 | 2.4 |
| (128, 4, 64) | 0.48 | 1.6 |
| (64, 4, 64) | 0.21 | 0.7 |
| (128, 4, 16) | 0.12 | 0.4 |

  Masking reduces the context-encoder cost, so these are upper bounds for the context pass; data loading/augmentation and the force-decomposition diagnostic are not included.

## Answers
**1. Tier C.** Make the **CPU reduced I-JEPA-style ViT primary** (e.g. dim 128, depth 4-6, patch 4, 64 tokens; 1.6-2.4 min/epoch measured) and the **ViT-Tiny/Ibex GPU run an optional extension that no must-have claim waits on.** The plan's "gated, with CPU fallback" inverts the risk: the fallback is the one that is certain to run. The reduced model is still a transformer encoder with patch-token masking and a transformer predictor, so brief R5 ("real JEPA training on a standard image benchmark") is met; describe it as a small ViT, not ViT-Tiny. A full ViT-Tiny (192, 12) costs 8 min/epoch on 8 CPUs: only a handful of runs at ~10 epochs fit, so it is Ibex-only. If Ibex is used, submit one speculative array as early as the pilot code exists (it may start in days, or not), `ht-resource track` it, and treat it as a bonus.
**2. Grid.** The order of magnitude is right in *runs*, wrong to set it before the pilot in *cost*. Using the measured (128, 4, 64) config: one-factor sweeps around the baseline, 5 values x 3-4 factors = ~15-20 distinct configs (the baseline cell is shared, do not count it per factor) x 3 seeds x ~8 epochs (~1.5k steps) = 45-60 runs x ~13 min = **~10-13 job-hours on 8 CPUs**, plus ~10 interventions (~2 h) and 2-3 full-length runs (100 epochs ~ 2.7 h each at (128,4,64)). That is feasible on `local-compute` with 2-3 jobs in parallel, subject to shared host load (60 CPUs placed on 40 at check time, so expect slower than 8 dedicated cores). A full 5x5x3 cross of all five factors is not meant and not affordable; keep it one-factor-at-a-time plus the interventions. Model-time budget (1e6 s) and turns (800) are not the binding constraint for compute that runs in jobs; the binding ones are **the length of the wait for each sweep and the number of re-runs after a change**, so freeze the E3 config and the metric code (rank, std, force decomposition) after the pilot, and post the grid once. The patch-8 (16-token) variant (0.4 min/epoch) is a cheap debugging config only: mask ratio is too coarse at 16 tokens for the mask sweep.
**3. Theory.** Feasible as one core proposition set (M1) + numerical verification; **keep M2 but second priority and conditional.** M2 closed forms need a common eigenbasis; random patch masking on a non-circular grid mixes Fourier modes, so state M2 as exact for the circulant/torus case (or per-mode mean-field) and verify numerically for the rest; if it is not closed-form by plan v1, present it as a derived approximation validated by E2, not cut and not claimed exact. Do not let M2 delay E1. The plan's own pilot note already says the static window count (P1/P2) is wrong for lambda>0 (a race); require the paper's headline criterion to be the time-dependent one with the static count only as a bound, and have the theory state it in a form that can be computed from (data statistics, hyperparameters) alone so R2 ("predict rank from data and set-up quantities") is met.

## Things in the plan that cannot meet the objective as written (to fix in plan v1)
a. **E3 test (i) is not an a-priori prediction.** Counting directions with measured drive > decay early in training uses quantities from the run itself. It is a valid *early-training diagnostic* (label it so), but the R4 forward predictions in E3 must be the heuristics' predicted **direction and location of the transition** (e.g. predicted critical weight decay, predictor LR/scale, mask ratio, EMA) committed before the sweep, with a pre-stated tolerance. Define "survives"/"collapsed" thresholds (e.g. eigenvalue relative to the top one; effective-rank cut) and the success criterion for a prediction **before** the sweeps; without that the claims are unfalsifiable in the grading sense.
b. **Optimizer.** The theory is gradient flow; I-JEPA uses AdamW, which rescales per-parameter growth and weight decay and changes the near-collapse rates. The force decomposition in E3 is only meaningful if the optimizer is accounted for. Require a **plain-SGD(+momentum) baseline arm in E3** (or the derivation for Adam-type updates) and report both; otherwise a mismatch cannot be attributed to nonlinearity.
c. **Normalisation.** I-JEPA applies LayerNorm to the target encoder output and the ViT has LayerNorm inside; "small embeddings near the collapsed point" is then not reachable in the usual sense. The plan must say how E3 handles it (an arm without target normalisation, or a theory statement for the normalised case); this is an expected failure source for C5.
d. **Date cutoff, tooling.** PyPI default for torch is 2.14.1 (2026-09-30); 2.14.0 is 2026-09-02; the latest permitted release is **2.13.0 (2026-07-08)** (checked on PyPI JSON). The same applies to every dependency: pin versions released on or before 2026-09-01 and record the pin file. My own venv has 2.14.1 installed from the default index: do not copy it.
e. **Data access.** `local-compute` has no network: fetch CIFAR-10 into /hutian beforehand (the canonical host redirects; Hugging Face/OpenML are on the allowed list).
f. **Evaluation gap.** Linear-probe accuracy after ~10 epochs of short training will be low and mostly informative relatively; plan the probe/kNN on the full-length runs only and say so. State the baseline reference accuracy (known-good recipe at this scale) so "rank differences tied to quality" has a comparator.
g. Everything else in the claims map is in scope. C4 (theory-guided rescue) is the first thing to cut if E3 slips; C5 should not be cut (it covers honest negative results).

## Go / no-go
- **GO:** E1, E2 (CPU, cheap) now; E3 pilot on `local-compute` with the reduced ViT; sweeps after the pilot grid is posted to me (I will reply with a bounded go/no-go).
- **NO-GO as a dependency:** any must-have result requiring Ibex GPUs. Ibex is optional/speculative.

