# v4: scoping for the extension objective (idea-review-repo#6), written before the plan for it exists

Basis: objective #6, the delivered paper (`paper/sections/discussion.tex` limitations, `paper/CLAIMS.md`), `story-repo` plan v1.2 and `assignment.json` @ 865169b (both still describe the first paper), and measurements below. Nothing here is a result; judge the real plan against it when `story-repo` posts it.

## Measurements made this turn (2026-10-17)
- **Ibex GPU queue, from `sacct`, 51 jobs of the previous world (all <= 2 h limits, 1 GPU):** submit-to-start wait median **4.7 min**, max 48 min (e3armP 21 jobs: median 8, max 12; e3armT 13 jobs: median 15, max 48). Elapsed time of all of those jobs together: **8.5 GPU-job-hours** (this was the whole of E3 incl. pilots, 2 arms, follow-up). This supersedes the v2 estimate; the earlier correction in v3 stands. Basis: one evening, one account (pi-gaox, fair-share), pending queue on `gpu` was 915 jobs when checked, so waits can change; `ht-resource status` shows no Ibex quota (`limits` empty), the real cap is unknown.
- **local-compute:** host has 40 CPUs, 56 placed, 1-min load 18.7: oversubscribed, so the 10 s/step seen earlier under load, not the 0.75 s/step best case, is the planning figure for any CPU-only deep run.
- **Cutoff and tooling:** the date cutoff moved from 2026-09-01 to **2026-10-18** (tomorrow). Torch 2.14.1 (2026-09-30) and other September releases are now allowed; items first published after 2026-10-18 stay forbidden (the practical risk is a late-dated web search hit or preprint version).
- **ICLR 2027 template:** exists in `github.com/ICLR/Master-Template` (`iclr2027/`: `iclr2027_conference.{tex,sty,bst,bib}`, `math_commands.tex`, `natbib.sty`, `fancyhdr.sty`; one commit, 2026-09-01), reachable through research-network. `iclr.cc` and the call for papers are **not** reachable through the world's resources (CONNECT 403); the page limit, checklist or reproducibility/LLM-use rules for 2027 are therefore unverified by me and must be established by `venue-repo` from the template's own comments and web search, and listed as unestablished if not found. Do not assume the NeurIPS 9-page/checklist rules carry over.
- **Budget:** the previous world used 30,901 model-seconds and 97 turns for a complete paper; this world has 1,000,000 s and 800 turns. Model time and turns are not the binding constraint. The binding ones are (i) the wall-clock of each compute cycle and re-runs after a change, (ii) the 1800 s idle cut for a turn that only waits, and (iii) the paper's page budget.

## What the objective requires, and the main feasibility risks
The delivered paper has 11 figures, 5 tables and ~42 KB of appendix; the extension must still be one paper with one story that fits the venue's main-text limit.

1. **Too many directions.** The open list (nonlinear/deep encoders, realistic data, far-from-collapse training, Adam, normalisation, noise floor, design implications, ViT failures) is each a paper's worth. Pick at most **three** and give each an exit criterion. A bolt-on of five weakly tested items will not read as an advance and cannot be checked within the page limit.
2. **"Theory far from collapse" can end with no closed form.** Realistic outcomes in order of likelihood: (a) exact statements for conserved quantities/invariant manifolds and a stage-wise (capture, then saturation) description verified against the exact flow; (b) an approximation validated numerically; (c) nothing beyond the old near-collapse regime. State up front which of these the claim is allowed to be, and verify against exact population flow in the linear model first (CPU, seconds per run, as E1/E2).
3. **Held-out discipline.** The previous E1 was a development set and one correction was made after a failed test. For every new criterion: commit predictions (with thresholds and a tolerance) before the run that tests it, on a grid disjoint from the one used to build it. This is cheap on CPU and is the difference between a result and a fit.
4. **The ViT failures need diagnosis, not more sweeps.** Predictor, EMA and masking predictions failed in the ViT; repeating the same sweeps with the same step-0 rule only reproduces the failure. The affordable, informative route is a **bridge from the linear model to the ViT by removing one difference at a time** (optimiser SGD->Adam, target normalisation on/off, depth, nonlinearity, token structure), each rung small and CPU or one short GPU job, with the theory's prediction for that rung stated first. That gives either an explanation or a located failure, both reportable.
5. **Optimiser and normalisation are the two largest known gaps between theory and practice** (SGD/gradient-flow vs AdamW; no LN vs LN on the target). Derivations for them are feasible in the linear/deep-linear model (sign-descent or per-parameter-rescaled early dynamics; normalised-target fixed points) and cheap to check; they are the most direct route to "what the theory says about practical heuristics". Rank them above new large-scale experiments.
6. **Compute for realistic experiments.** At the measured numbers, 8.5 GPU-h bought the whole previous E3. A second realistic stage of **~5-10x that** (40-90 GPU-h: more seeds or a second dataset such as CIFAR-100, or the 50-epoch runs) fits at 5-15 min waits if jobs are short (<= 2 h), resumable, array-submitted, tracked. Not feasible: ImageNet-1k pretraining, V-JEPA/video, or any run needing > 12 h GPU on one job. A second dataset must be fetched through an allowed host (Hugging Face/OpenML) and checked, not assumed (ImageNet subsets are typically gated).
7. **Inherited artifacts.** All old numbers come from the old code; `paper/numbers.tex` and the figure scripts read fixed paths in `results/`. Changing the metric code or renaming paths invalidates them silently. Keep the old results and code paths **unchanged and additive** (new work in new directories, e.g. `results/x1/...`), record the code commit for new results, and mark in the paper which numbers are inherited and which are new. If old code must change, re-run the affected old check and say so.
8. **Page budget and "what the extension adds".** The objective asks to keep what holds, revise what changes, and show the addition. That needs a bounded set of old results moved to the appendix and a short table "paper v1 vs this paper" decided before figures are made, not after. `paper-repo` and `figtab-repo` cost is real: plan for a restructure, not an append.
9. **Prior work window moved.** The old context map is cut at 2026-09-01. New allowed material (2026-09-01 .. 2026-10-18) must be swept by `idea-repo` before the novelty claims for the chosen directions are fixed; claims that the old paper credited as new may now have appeared elsewhere. The cutoff is a hard line on both ends.
10. **Venue switch.** `assignment.json` item 1 says NeurIPS 2026 template and checklist; the objective requires the ICLR 2027 template. The assignment text, `venue-repo`'s checker (`tools/check_submission.py`, built for NeurIPS) and the paper's `.sty` and checklist all change. This is a delivery gap that is cheap now and expensive at review time.

## Recommended bounds for the plan (to be revised against the real plan)
- **Core (must):** (A) theory extended beyond the near-collapse window: capture and saturation stage and/or the finite-batch noise floor, in the linear/deep-linear model, with exact-flow verification on a held-out grid; (B) optimiser (Adam) and target-normalisation effects in the same model, with a stated prediction and a test; (C) the linear-to-ViT bridge of risk 4, with one realistic-model confirmation run per rung that the theory claims to cover and an honest statement of the rest.
- **Design implication (should, cut first if C slips):** one or two prospective rules derived from A/B (e.g. a critical weight decay or predictor-lr scaling), tested as predictions in the controlled model and once in the ViT.
- **Cut order:** second dataset, then design-rule ViT test, then extra seeds beyond 3; never cut the held-out verification in A/B or the reporting of failed predictions.
- **Compute caps (planning):** controlled-model work on `local-compute` (CPU, minutes per job); ViT-stage <= ~90 GPU-h in total, each job <= 2 h, packed seeds, resumable, tracked, fallback trigger as in v3 (no start 60 min after submission). Ask `ht-resource status` before a large array.
- **Go/no-go gates I will give on request:** post the grid and the prediction file path before launch, as before; I answer with a bounded go/no-go.

## Judgement
Feasible at the bounded scope above; not feasible if all open directions are taken, or if the paper must keep the first paper's full content in the main text and add a full second study. The two things that most threaten the objective are an unresolved page/structure plan and a theory direction with no verifiable statement; both should be settled in plan v2, before experiments launch.

