## What this means for E10 (gaps to close before its pre-registration; none blocks A-C)
1. **Prospective vs retrospective.** Scoring the race on their published 810 cells, or on the jannis predictor-scale pattern (their Tab. 16), is retrospective even when the prediction is computed from inputs only, because the outcomes are public and were seen by the project. Say so in the registration and the paper. A claim of a pre-registered test is available only for runs executed after the prediction file is committed (new seeds, or cells off their grid, e.g. weight decay values they did not run). Do not call the agreement row a test of the race: the plan already predicts race = mu-wd there, so it is a consistency check of the implementation, and a tie is not a win.
2. **Informative cells must be selected from inputs, before any run.** Parts (a) (weight decay where race and window differ) and (b) are informative only where the two predictions differ. Register, computed from data statistics and settings alone, the number of cells and the minimum predicted difference in surviving directions at which the two criteria count as differing, and report the cells where they coincide as ties. If the pilot shows the extended grid has fewer than a registered number of differing cells, the extension is stated as not discriminating, not enlarged until it is.
3. **Metric mapping.** Their ERank is exp(entropy) of normalised singular values; the delivered scoring is centred RankMe and a thresholded count, and the race predicts a count. Register one primary statistic for race vs mu (rank MAE of the thresholded count and Spearman with CI on the same runs, both criteria on the same cells) and the map from the predicted count to ERank, and report their ERank-Spearman (0.906 / 0.905 / 0.619) as they define it. Take their mu readout as published (p25, median, p75 at epoch 0, their r_tight count) rather than choosing the best readout after seeing outcomes.
4. **Cap and cost.** Cost is unmeasured, so: pilot one cell (40 epochs, jannis, WANDB disabled) on local-compute at the loaded rate and on Ibex if CPU is too slow, record seconds per run, then size E10 so that it fits **<= 10 GPU-h-equivalent and <= ~400 runs in total** (or less if the pilot says so), with 3 seeds on the extension cells (their seeds are 3), and register the cell list. If a run is longer than ~10 CPU-min, move E10 to Ibex under the v3 job rules (<= 2 h, resumable, tracked).
5. **Cut order.** Add E10: the P2 baselines (mu-count and mu-wd on E1b/E2 and as columns in E4-E8) are cheap and stay uncut, as does reporting the race's located failures. E10 parts (a)/(b) are cut after optional scale and before the ViT design-rule rows; P3's retrospective scoring on their public CSV (zero compute) is never cut, labelled as retrospective.
6. **P4 (ResidualPred ViT row).** The delivered image code is CC BY-NC 4.0: reimplement the identity-biased attention from the paper's description in the delivered ViT instead of copying it, keep the reimplementation's differences from the original listed, and keep author-identifying text of the repository out of the delivered supplementary code (anonymity). The "per-direction gain over time" must be an operational quantity registered before the instrumented run (for example top singular values of the predictor's feature-space map on a fixed batch, the quantity their K.1 reports), since a transformer predictor has no single matrix. The prediction file (time and set of surviving directions) is committed before the run, as the plan says. If the reimplementation cannot reproduce ResidualPred's published direction (higher rank than the standard predictor) at the delivered recipe, report that as a reimplementation outcome, not as a test of either theory.
7. **Pin.** Pin their code by commit (`3176215`) and record the environment of the E10 runs; if it is not the E3 environment, record the difference (their range includes torch 2.5.1, so use it).

## Go / no-go (additions to v5)
- **GO now:** the P2 baselines (mu-count, mu-wd) on E1b/E2 and in E4-E6 (inputs-only computation); retrospective P3 scoring on their public CSV; a single-cell E10 timing pilot (<= 1 CPU-h).
