# v11: Feasibility of the writing-only objective (idea-review-repo#11)

Basis: the delivery assembled at paper-repo 771f5a9 (`ht-role delivery`, read, compiled `paper/main.pdf` opened with PyMuPDF). Measurements, not estimates: 47 pages in total; the 9-page main text is full (page 9 ~910 words, References start on page 10); `sections/*.tex` hold ~19.3k words, of which ~11.1k are appendix (`appendix.tex`, `appendix_ext.tex`). Every number in the text comes from `numbers.tex`, generated from `results/`. Nothing was run.

## Judgement: feasible, with no compute. The limits are the page count and the number macros.
1. **No compute, no new GPU or CPU budget.** The objective forbids new experiments; all earlier go/no-go bounds are closed and are not reopened. No resource launch is needed or licensed. Redrawing a figure is allowed only for readability, from existing `results/` through `code/figures/` (milliseconds of CPU).
2. **The 9-page limit is the binding constraint.** The main text is already full. Any added sentence (a clearer claim ladder, a figure interpretation, a statement that corrects an over-claim) must be paid for by a cut or by moving material to the appendix. Moving material is allowed (appendices are supplementary), but a claim that stays in the main text must keep its evidence there or an explicit pointer. Check `tools/check_submission.py` after every layout change; do not trim margins, fonts or float spacing.
3. **Text numbers must stay generated.** Rewording a sentence must keep the `\num…` macros from `numbers.tex`; a hand-typed value is the main way a rewrite silently changes a result. After each pass, grep the diff for digits outside macros. "Change the text to the evidence" means weakening or restating a claim, never re-deriving a number.
4. **Over/under-claim fixes are bounded by the registered verdicts.** Retrospective or post hoc results (E10/P3, E6 post hoc column), the registered fail of rung 3, the E7 flag-only wording and E7-G (no family difference *detectable at 3 seeds*, not "no effect") must keep their labels. A rewrite that smooths these into a cleaner story is a defect; the claim maps in `CLAIMS.md` are the standard.
5. **Scope limit: 47 pages and ~19k words cannot all be rewritten in one pass.** Priority order: abstract, introduction (claim and contribution list), Sec. 5-7 (experiments and extension: claims, interpretation of each figure/table, captions), discussion and limitations, related work (each citation in a sentence about the cited work, esp. 2610.02344), then appendices only for consistency with the main text. Cut first: appendix polishing, then stylistic edits to the theory proofs.
6. **Risks for the process.** (a) Eight positions running at once on one text: `paper/` has one owner (paper-repo); figtab-repo's captions in `paper/figures` and `paper/tables` must not be edited in place by the paper owner, request them. (b) `CHANGES.md` must record what changed and why for the writing pass, in addition to the extension's account, or the review cannot check it. (c) Cutoff: no work first published after 2026-10-18 may be added while fixing citations.
7. **Budget.** 30,000 model-seconds and 150 turns for the world; the previous world used 25,327 s over 149 turns for the whole study. A full-text rewrite by several positions should be done in two or three passes with one review round each; do not plan for more than three `internal-review-repo` decisions (the world ends unresolved after 3).

## Go / no-go
- **GO:** the writing pass as scoped above; nothing to bound in compute.
- **NO-GO:** anything needing a new run, a changed result, a new direction, or a main-text page above 9.
